2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8124x5416+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff6%2F42%2F04e44b504ab1a96d22c59d2a7b51%2Fgettyimages-2298960624.jpg" class="img" alt="A driver look at his overturned truck in a flooded parking lot on Saturday morning in Tiger Point, Fla., close to where Hurricane Isaias made landfall Friday night." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 A driver look at his overturned truck in a flooded parking lot on Saturday morning in Tiger Point, Fla., close to where Hurricane Isaias made landfall Friday night.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Ronaldo Schemidt/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Emergency crews in Florida and Alabama are surveying damage wrought by Hurricane Isaias, after the storm bashed into the Gulf Coast as a Category 2 storm Friday night. At least two people have died from storm-related incidents, NPR has confirmed.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Isaias brought 105 mph winds onshore, snapping trees and knocking out electricity. Officials warn that flash flood threats persist, but further inland.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-147632-100" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESG-S1-147632-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;One death occurred in Escambia County, in the far west of the Florida Panhandle. Shortly before 9 p.m. local time Friday, a rescue team found a person pinned under a large tree, according to Sgt. Melony Peterson of the Escambia County Sheriff's Office.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"The victim was inside the home when the tree collapsed," Peterson said via email.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Another death was reported in Jefferson County, Ala., which includes Birmingham.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"We did have a storm-related fatality overnight," Jefferson County Emergency Management Agency Director Jim Coker told NPR. "A tree did fall on the house," Coker added. "It was a single-family residence."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-147636" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 6000;         --source-height: 4000;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6000x4000+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Ff9%2F97%2Ff6d48af34af5b7aa2fdcd4251062%2Fgettyimages-2299531632.jpg" class="img" alt="Hurricane Isaias forced a Waffle House restaurant to shut down when it passed through Fort Walton Beach, Florida. Isaias made landfall as the season&amp;#039;s first Atlantic hurricane." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Hurricane Isaias forced a Waffle House restaurant to shut down when it passed through Fort Walton Beach, Florida. Isaias made landfall as the season's first Atlantic hurricane.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Joe Raedle/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;As of 11:50 a.m. ET Saturday, about 780,000 power customers remained without power in Florida, Alabama and Georgia, according to the monitoring site &lt;a href="http://poweroutage.us/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Poweroutage.us&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. That figure had been closer to 900,000 earlier Saturday.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;In Florida, the worst outages were in counties such as Santa Rosa; Holmes; Washington; and Walton – all of which had more than 80% of their residents without power, Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis said in a morning news conference.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The governor said some 16,000 utility workers were busy trying to get power returned to homeowners.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"Last night in the wee hours of the morning, those crews began working as early as 3 a.m.," DeSantis said. He added that teams were also working to remove trees blocking main roads -- and that both I-10 and Route 98 were open as of Saturday morning, along with all state bridges.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-147637" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 7516;         --source-height: 5011;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/7516x5011+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fc2%2Fe9%2Fc252c3634b58bc702d2bd76fae56%2Fgettyimages-2298960666.jpg" class="img" alt="An uprooted tree is seen in Gulf Breeze, Fla., on Saturday. The southeastern U.S. faced a high risk of flooding as Isaias, weakened from a hurricane to a post-tropical cyclone, moved northward." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 An uprooted tree is seen in Gulf Breeze, Fla., on Saturday. The southeastern U.S. faced a high risk of flooding  as Isaias, weakened from a hurricane to a post-tropical cyclone, moved northward.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Ronaldo Schemidt/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Isaias made landfall near Destin, Fla., as a Category 2 hurricane around 8:30 p.m. local time Friday night, packing 105 mph winds, according to the&lt;a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/al09/al092026.update.10100129.shtml" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;National Hurricane Center&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. It brought a storm surge of some 5 feet -- short of the 6 to 9 feet forecasters had said was possible if landfall coincided with peak high tide. It arrived roughly two hours before high tide in Destin.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-147632-101" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESG-S1-147632-101" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Isaias was downgraded to a post-tropical storm hours after making landfall. By 5 a.m. local time on Saturday, its sustained winds had dropped to 35 mph, as the system sped its way through Alabama.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"Multiple trees and power lines are down across the county,"&lt;a href="https://x.com/EMAJeffCoAL/status/2108854301070131475" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;tweeted&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; the emergency management agency in Jefferson County, Ala. It told residents to "avoid unnecessary travel if possible while crews clear roads and restore service."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;But the storm's effects extended for hundreds of miles. By around 9:30 a.m. Saturday, the system sent a long band of powerful thunder storms up the center of Georgia. And as the&lt;a href="https://www.fpren.org/2026-10-09/isaias-makes-landfall-near-destin-widespread-impacts-continue" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Florida Public Radio Emergency Network&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; notes, forecasters warn that tornadoes could spin off of the system, causing further havoc far from the Gulf Coast.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
